PuTTY for RISC OS

PuTTY is a terminal program for logging in to other computers over a network:
SSH (the secure shell), Telnet, Rlogin, or a plain connection.  This is a RISC OS
port of PuTTY 0.85 by Simon Tatham and others.

Starting
--------
Double-click !PuTTY to put its icon on the icon bar.  Click on the icon (or choose
New session... from its menu) to open the Connect box.  Menu on the icon gives:

  Info          about this program (its version and licence)
  Help...       this file
  New session...  the Connect box
  Sessions      the sessions you have saved: choose one to connect straight away
                (Adjust-click keeps the menu open)
  Quit

The Connect box has:

  Host name   the computer to log in to, e.g. pi.local or 192.168.1.5
  Port        22 for SSH, 23 for Telnet
  Connection  SSH, Telnet, Rlogin or Raw
  Login name  the name to log in as (you are asked if you leave it empty)
  Key file    a private key (.ppk) to log in with instead of a password:
              drag the key's file onto the Connect box
  Session     a name to save these settings under; Save, Load and Delete work
              on saved sessions, and Sessions... lists them

Settings... opens PuTTY's full configuration dialogue: choose a heading in the list
on the left (click a heading with a + to see what is under it) and change what you
need.  Its Open button starts the session.  Dragging a file onto a file box (a key
file, a log file) fills in its name.

Click Open to connect.  PuTTY asks you in the terminal window for a password if
one is needed.  The first time you connect to a computer it shows the host key's
fingerprint: choose Accept to remember it, or Connect Once to trust it just this
time.  Adjust-click Open to keep the Connect box on screen.

The terminal window
-------------------
Select-drag (or double or triple click) selects text, and Adjust-click extends the
selection.  Selected text is copied to the RISC OS clipboard at once; Shift-Insert
pastes the clipboard (Ctrl-Insert copies).  Mouse wheel and Shift-Page Up/Down
scroll back through the output.  A text file dropped on the window is pasted.
The left Alt key is Meta, as in a Unix terminal (Alt-F sends Escape then F); the right Alt
key still types the special characters of your keyboard layout.

Menu on the window gives Copy, Paste, Special command (Break, signals, ...),
Event log (what PuTTY is doing and why a connection failed), Change settings
(the configuration dialogue again, for the running session: Apply puts the changes
to use at once), Restart session, Reset terminal, Clear scrollback, Duplicate
session (the same settings and window size) and New session, and Info.  Dragging the
window's size box changes the number of rows and columns; Change settings shows
the new size and Save in its Session panel keeps it.

From the command line (for example from an Obey file) PuTTY takes the options of
the other versions: putty -ssh -P 22 -l user -i keyfile host, or putty -load session.

Random numbers
--------------
PuTTY needs good random numbers to make the keys of an SSH session.  RISC OS has no
built-in source, so PuTTY uses the CryptRandom module.  It is normally already in your
!System (try  *Info System:Modules.CryptRand  in a command window) and PuTTY loads it
itself when it needs it.  If PuTTY warns that it cannot find it, install the CryptRandom
package with !PackMan (System section; or see
http://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~theom/riscos/crypto/ ) and start PuTTY again.
Without the module PuTTY still works but its keys are weaker than they should be (set
the system variable PuTTY$NoEntropyWarning to silence the box).

Command-line tools
------------------
The Tools directory inside !PuTTY holds command-line programs: puttygen, psftp and pscp.
Once the Filer has seen !PuTTY (or you have run it) they can be run by name from a task
window or the command line (F12).  Options such as -i keyfile, -l user, -P port, -pw
password and -batch are as in the other PuTTY tools; "psftp -h" and "pscp -h" list them.

psftp and pscp: copying files
-----------------------------
psftp and pscp copy files to and from another computer over SSH.  pscp copies in one
command, like cp:

  pscp pi@host:/home/pi/notes.txt Docs.notes/txt     fetch a file
  pscp Docs.notes/txt pi@host:/home/pi/              send a file
  pscp -r pi@host:/home/pi/projects ADFS::HardDisc4.$.Work    a whole directory
  pscp -p ...    keep the file dates      pscp -ls pi@host:/home/pi    list a directory

psftp is interactive, like an FTP client: "psftp pi@host", then at the psftp> prompt
ls, cd, pwd, get, put (with -r for directories), mget, mput (wildcards), mkdir, rmdir,
rm, mv, lcd and lpwd for the local directory, help, quit.  The cursor Up and Down keys
bring back earlier commands.  "psftp -b file" runs the commands in a file.

Local names are RISC OS ones, as the Filer shows them (drag a file to the task window to
type its name).  A name before a colon is a filing system or path variable (ADFS::4.$.x,
Choices:PuTTY.x) and the file is local; anything else with a colon is host:path (write
user@host: if a host name is short enough to look like a filing system).  The names on
the other computer are converted as the files cross:

  notes.txt        notes/txt          a dot in a Unix name is a slash here, and back
  my file.txt      my<hard space>file/txt
  a:b$c            a_b_c              characters RISC OS names cannot have become _
  picture,aff      picture (Draw)     ",xxx" at the end of a name is the file's type

A file you fetch gets its type from a ",xxx" suffix on its name if it has one; otherwise
from its extension if it is a common one (txt, html, png, jpg, gif, pdf, zip, csv, ...);
otherwise Text or Data according to what is in it.  A file you send gets ",xxx" added to
its name unless it is Text or Data (set the system variable PuTTY$SftpNoTypes to leave
that off).  File dates are kept.

puttygen
--------
puttygen makes SSH keys and converts them.  PuTTY reads only its own .ppk files, so a
key made by OpenSSH (ssh-keygen) or ssh.com has to be converted first:

  puttygen -t ed25519 -o MyKey            make a new key (it asks for a passphrase)
  puttygen id_rsa -o MyKey                convert an OpenSSH private key to PuTTY's form
  puttygen MyKey -O public-openssh        print the public key, ready to be added to
                                          ~/.ssh/authorized_keys on the other computer
  puttygen MyKey -l                       print its fingerprint
  puttygen MyKey -P                       change the passphrase
  puttygen MyKey -C "new comment"         change the comment
  puttygen MyKey -O private-openssh -o id_ed25519   write it out for OpenSSH
  puttygen --help                         every option

Use RISC OS file names.  A Unix name such as id_rsa.pub arrives on RISC OS as
id_rsa/pub, and is typed that way.  The files puttygen writes are Text files.  To
change a key in place, name only the key (puttygen MyKey -P).

It asks for passphrases without showing them.  For scripts, --old-passphrase FILE and
--new-passphrase FILE read the passphrase from the first line of a text file, and
answering with just Return saves a key without a passphrase.  ed25519 keys are made at
once; an RSA key takes a minute or more on a slow machine (-t rsa -b 3072).

A new key needs good random numbers.  puttygen uses the CryptRandom module (see Random
numbers) and will not make a key without it: it says so and stops.  A file of random
bytes made on another computer can be used instead: --random-device FILE.

A key saved with a passphrase is scrambled with a deliberately memory-hungry method that
needs about 10 MB.  If puttygen stops with "Out of memory", give the task window more
memory, or ask for a cheaper one with --ppk-param memory=1024.

Files
-----
Settings, saved sessions and the host keys PuTTY has accepted are kept in the
PuTTY directory of <Choices$Write>.

